  "raw_label": "-1",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "Food Inspector vs M.Gunendran on 15 June, 2020 Author: P.V.Kunhikrishnan Bench: P.V.Kunhikrishnan In The High Court Of Kerala At Ernakulam Present The Honourable Mr. Justice P.V.Kunhikrishnan Monday, The 15Th Day Of June 2020 / 25Th Jyaishta, 1942 CRL.A.No.203 Of 2005(C) Against The Order/Judgment In Cc 47/2001 Dated 31-01-2004 Of Judicial Magistrate Of First Class -I, Kannur Appellant/Complainant: Food Inspector, Kannur Municipality ,Rep. By Public Prosecutor, High Court Of Kerala. By Adv. Public Prosecutor Respondents/Accused: 1 M.Gunendran Manager ,Wheat House, Civil Station Road,, Kannur. 2 M.N.Sunil S/O.Bharathan,Wheat House, Civil Station Road,, Kannur. 3 E.Vasudevan S/O.P.Kunhiraman Nair ,Cheriya Veedu, Edakkad P.O. R1 By Adv. Sri.K.Babu R1 By Adv. Sri.A.Mohamed Mustaque R1 By Adv. Sri.N.Nagaresh R1 By Adv. Sri.V.Rajagopal R1 By Adv. Sri.M.K.Sumod R3 By Adv. Sri.M.V.Amaresan Other Present: Sr.Pp. Sri.B.Jayasurya Food Inspector vs M.Gunendran on 15 June, 2020 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/112564419/ 1 This Criminal Appeal Having Been Finally Heard On 15.06.2020, The Court On The Same Day Delivered The Following: Crl.Appeal No.203/2005 2 Judgment",
    "facts": "Dated this the 15th day of June 2020 This appeal is filed by the Food Inspector, Kannur Municipality against the acquittal order passed by the Judicial First Class Magistrate I, Kannur in C.C. No.47/2001. The respondents, herein, are the accused in the above case. (Hereinafter the parties are mentioned by their rank before the trial court). 2. The Food Inspector, Kannur filed a complaint against the respondents/accused for prosecuting them under Sections 16(1-A)(i) r/w Section 2(ia) (a)(j) & (m) and Section 7(1) of Prevention of Food Adulteration Act 1954 and Rule 29 and Rule 5 Appendix B-A-16-06 of Prevention of Food Adulteration Rules 1955. (hereinafter referred to as the PFA Act & Rules respectively). 3. The case of the complaint, in brief, is that the Food Inspector inspected the hotel by name \"Wheat House\" running at door No.206, Civil Station Road, Kannur on 26.8.1997 at about 4.50 p.m. After introducing himself as the Food Inspector he purchased one bottle of Tomato Sauce from a set of six bottles. After complying the legal formalities, he took a sample from the tomato sauce and prepared a mahassar. Thereafter, according to the Food inspector, he forwarded one set of samples for getting an expert opinion. The Public Analyst reported that the sample does not conform to the standards prescribed in the tomato sauce under the PFA Rules. Hence the complaint. 4. To substantiate the case, PW1 to PW4 were examined on the side of the complainant. Exts.P1 to P20 are the exhibits marked by the prosecution. One witness was examined as DW1 on the side of the defence, who is none other than the first accused. Ext.D1 is also marked on the side of the defence. Aggrieved by the above acquittal order, this Criminal Appeal is filed.",
    "arguments": "A Division Bench of this Court in Rasheed N.K. Vs. Food Inspector, Thalassery and another (2016 (2) KLT 390) held that the prosecution under the PFA Act 1954 will not lie unless the laboratory for the analysis by Public Analysts is not defined and the method of analysis are also defined under Section 23 (1-A) (ee) and Section 23 (1-A) (hh) of the PFA Act. Section 23(1) of the PFA Act says that the Central Government may, after consultation with the Committee and after previous publication, in the official gazette make Rules to carry out the provisions of this Act. Section 23 (1-A) (ee) and (hh) says that, in particular and without prejudice to the generality of the foregoing power such Rules may provide for all or any of the following matters namely-\"defining the laboratories where samples of article of food for adulterants may be analyzed by public analysts under this \"Act and defining the method of analysis\".",
    "petitioner_arguments": "",
    "respondent_arguments": "",
    "other_lawyer_arguments": "A Division Bench of this Court in Rasheed N.K. Vs. Food Inspector, Thalassery and another (2016 (2) KLT 390) held that the prosecution under the PFA Act 1954 will not lie unless the laboratory for the analysis by Public Analysts is not defined and the method of analysis are also defined under Section 23 (1-A) (ee) and Section 23 (1-A) (hh) of the PFA Act. Section 23(1) of the PFA Act says that the Central Government may, after consultation with the Committee and after previous publication, in the official gazette make Rules to carry out the provisions of this Act. Section 23 (1-A) (ee) and (hh) says that, in particular and without prejudice to the generality of the foregoing power such Rules may provide for all or any of the following matters namely-\"defining the laboratories where samples of article of food for adulterants may be analyzed by public analysts under this \"Act and defining the method of analysis\"."
  },
